--TEST--
The four types with no text of their own — `bytes`, an `array<T>`, an enum case and a call returning `void` — are refused where they are joined rather than rendered as something plausible, each with the help that names what to write instead, and `.`, `.=` and an interpolated piece take the same refusal because they are one conversion site, `rule:types/string-is-utf8`, `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:expressions/string-operators
// Four types have no text of their own, and each is refused where it is
// written rather than rendered as something plausible. The help names what
// to write instead, because that choice belongs to the program: which
// encoding the octets are in, which of the array's values matter, what an
// enum case should be called. `.`, `.=` and an interpolated piece are the
// same conversion site and take the same refusal.
enum Suit { Clubs, Hearts }

class Audit {
    public static function record(string $message): void {}
}

bytes $b = "hi" as bytes;
array<int> $xs = [1, 2];

string $fromBytes = "x" . $b;
string $fromArray = "x" . $xs;
string $fromCase = "x" . Suit::Clubs;
string $fromVoid = "x" . Audit::record("m");

string $line = "x";
$line .= $b;

string $filled = "x{$xs}";
echo "unreached\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0707]: `bytes` has no string form
   --> %s:18:27
   |
18 | string $fromBytes = "x" . $b;
   |                           ^^ converted to `string` here
   = help: `rule:types/conversion` makes that conversion explicit, because which encoding the octets are in is a decision — `$b as string`

error[E0707]: `array<int>` has no string form
   --> %s:19:27
   |
19 | string $fromArray = "x" . $xs;
   |                           ^^^ converted to `string` here
   = help: an `array<T>` has no text of its own; render it — `Core\Json::encode($a)` — or build the string from its elements

error[E0707]: `Suit` has no string form
   --> %s:20:26
   |
20 | string $fromCase = "x" . Suit::Clubs;
   |                          ^^^^^^^^^^^ converted to `string` here
   = help: an enum case is a named integer (`rule:enums/no-class-machinery`), not text — `$case as int`, or a member of your own that names it

error[E0707]: `void` has no string form
   --> %s:21:26
   |
21 | string $fromVoid = "x" . Audit::record("m");
   |                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ converted to `string` here
   = help: a call that returns `void` has no value at all, so there is nothing here to print

error[E0707]: `bytes` has no string form
   --> %s:24:10
   |
24 | $line .= $b;
   |          ^^ converted to `string` here
   = help: `rule:types/conversion` makes that conversion explicit, because which encoding the octets are in is a decision — `$b as string`

error[E0707]: `array<int>` has no string form
   --> %s:26:21
   |
26 | string $filled = "x{$xs}";
   |                     ^^^ converted to `string` here
   = help: an `array<T>` has no text of its own; render it — `Core\Json::encode($a)` — or build the string from its elements

